From: PATRICK WILKEN <X91007@pitvax.xx.rmit.edu.au>
Date: Mon, 12 Dec 1994 08:42:20 +1000
Subject: [#94-12-447] EVOL: Is enhancement like peeling an onion?

>Curt Adams says
>
>reptilian = brainstem
>mammalian = limbic system
>3rd layer = cerebral cortex
>
>The cortex does complex processing, but does not create conciousness, at
>least not by itself.

I think you have the terms a little confused: reptilian usually refers
to the limibic system; mammalian to the cortex.

While I am happy to accept that the brain, like the rest of the body, has 
accumulated bits-and-pieces over the course of evolution. However it
would be wrong to say that the cortex does complex processing and the
limbic system doesn't. They just do different things. 

My original was question a little bit tongue in cheek: there is no part
of the brain that has been clearly linked with consciousness. We don't
know where the conscious parts of the brain are located or even if its
sensible to think of some parts of the brain as conscious and other
parts not. Though certainly some processes are carried on
non-consciousnessly. Don't rule the limbic system out of the equation:
emotions play a significant part in conscious experience. 

Best wishes,

Patrick

