From: levy@cs.utk.edu
Date: Sun, 11 Dec 1994 17:19:00 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-12-448] ECON: The inevitability of Gibson

> 
> From: William_John@gnj.or.jp (William John)
> 
> I want to respond to whoever it was who wrote the most recent post about Gibson,

I wrote that post.

> 
> I've read a lot of Pynchon, Burroughs, and Dick, and Gibson's particular
> expression of 'doom' strikes home with me with much more immediacy.  In fact,
> for me, it was the very realism of Gibson's backdrop that made _Neuromancer_ and
> the others  so powerful for me, and which stuck with me much longer than the
> particulars of the plot and characters.  

Then I *highly* recommend Chandler.  I also recommend that you try and keep
your lines under 80 characters long (a problem I also had a while back).

> 
> Basically, I think Gibson has based his depiction of a future dystopia on the
> simple extrapolation of a few powerful trends which we can see around us quite
> clearly now.  Perhaps the most important of these, and the one with the most
> significant ramifications for Extropian thought, is the trend of increasing
> polarization of wealth in Western capitalist societies, particularly the United
> States.   The underlying cause of this trend, it seems to me, is a structural
> change in the economic structure of society.   In a nutshell, as we move to a
> information-based economy, with free international competition, highly educated
> or skilled people who work with information for a living (I believe that Toffler
> has referred to these people as 'symbol-manipulators' or something similar) are
> able to reap huge rewards by selling their skills to the highest bidder in the
> global marketplace, while unskilled or low-skill workers in an advanced society
> like the U.S. increasingly lose out in competition with low-wage workers in
> countries like China.  

With all due respect, I don't buy Toffler's ideas.  Years ago he made
a fortune wringing his hands about "future shock"; that having failed to
come to pass, he now tries to scare us with the old rich-get-richer/poor-
get-poorer story, this time cast in terms of "the information society".

Nor do I buy products from China, because that country uses slaves to
manufacture a large proportion of its goods.  Other than that, I have 
little sympathy for unskilled out-of-work US laborers.  Years of
protectionism and other forms of government interference (OSHA,
mandatory insurance, etc.) have made it extremely difficult to manufacture
goods in the US.  Now the unambitious work force that supported such
policies has started to pay the price.  Nature won't tolerate a 
vacuum, so the jobs move south or overseas.  

As far as an information econonomy goes, I'll repeat what I've said 
before on this List: hard times eventually come, and you can't eat 
(or drive, or fuel your car with, or stay warm inside) information.  
I hope that the corrupt assholes who run our government will eventually 
wake up to this fact and pull out some of the stops they've placed on our 
industry. Meantime, I recommend learning some Japanese (or Spanish, 
or Korean, or Mandarin), so you can communicate with your future employer.

> 
> I think this is the most important trend underpinning Gibson's dystopian vision.
> The symbol-manipulators are able to enjoy the good life in the bosom of the
> megacorporations, while everyone else loses out.  Gibson has simply portrayed
> some possible effects of such a situation.  It seems to me that conditions would
> be even worse under an anarcho-capitalist 'system' than Gibson portrays them,
> and I think that this is a point that Extropians should consider very carefully,
> especially as I'm reasonably certain that most of them are symbol-manipulators
> themselves.  I'm not sure if those who are espousing complete privatization of
> everything have considered just how ugly and brutal such a society could become
> in a situation where a small portion of the population were taking home a hugely
> disproportionate slice of the income pie.

History repeatedly fails to show a long-term correlation between (a) private
control of industry and (b) skewed wealth distribution.  Dressing up a
failed argument in "information society" terms doesn't change this fact.

Furthermore, as long as we drive cars, go to the toilet, and use other
mechanical systems, many skilled non-information jobs will continue to employ 
large numbers of people.  The listings for "Automobile Repairing & Service" 
take up 12 of the Yellow Pages in the town where I live (Knoxville, TN).
"Plumbing Contractors" takes up more than 10. "Computers - Software & Services"
takes up barely one.   Next time your pipes need fixing, tell me how much 
concern you have for the guy who manipulates wrenches instead of symbols,
after you pay his bill.

> 
> To those who will respond that there have always been rich and poor, I would
> reply that the information-based economy seems to be drastically reducing the
> ranks of the middle-class, along with a certain potential for bettering one's
> position if one begins at the bottom.  
> 
> Comments?

Really, I just don't see this happening.  People who failed to train for
jobs now in demand (word-processing, programming, health care) find
themselves out of work.  People who didn't, don't.  I'm sure that the
same thing happened at the start of the Industrial Revolution.  Whether
this trend ends up meaning a long-term reduction in the ranks of the
middle class remains a matter for speculation.  The evidence argues
to the contrary.  And if you really care about people beginning at the
bottom, vote against the politicians who support minimum wage, mandatory
insurance plans, drug prohibition, and other coercive policies that keep
the poor in poverty.

-- 

I wrote this in E-prime.


Simon D. Levy			levy@cs.utk.edu

