Date: Sun, 11 Dec 1994 14:27:04 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-449] ETHICS: "Sleeping" for success & virtue

First off, I want to thank Max for writing such a long and intensive
statement of his thoughts on this subject. He has obviously spent a
significant amount of time working out his current position.

>> As a relativist (sorry Reilly), I would examine each situation in context
>> to determine if I should except the offer. Factors I would evaluate would
>> be: What are the chances of negative repercussions if the facts became
>> public? Does this person interest me sexually in someway? Conversely, am I
>> repelled by this offer or person? How much do I stand to gain? Would this
>> effect my autonomy? And various other things that would be unique to the
>> situation. Can I be truthful in this situation (this mostly relates to
>> reason one)?
>
>The problem I have with your procedure is that is makes one's current and
>particular desires all-important.

You seem to be under the incorrect impression that I don't take the long
term view of consequences. This mostly relates to my first evualuation
factor: "What are the chances of negative repercussions if the facts became
public?" Fraud, lies, and taking advantage of people hurt you in the long
run. My current desires include living a very long time and being happy
through that period. This is factored into my ethical decision making
process.

>I espouse an ethics of virtue (as
>distinguished from a consequentialist or deontological morality). On an
>ethics of virtue, character is primary rather than actions, outcomes, or
>abstract rules. The virtuous person is one who lives in accordance with their
>nature. Living virtuously means _flourishing_. The basic moral facts are
>those about what is constitutively and instrumentally needed for life as the
>kind of being we are, that is, for a flourishing life. (Virtue approaches to
>ethics have long been unpopular, until very recently, though Aristotle and
>Nietzsche can be regarded as holding virtue-based views.)

How do you define or choose a virtue? Doesn't the statement "Living
virtuously means _flourishing_" indicate that virtues are chosen on long
term consequences?
This is especially true for people who expect to live an extremely long time.

Isn't character a form of reputation capital? You act in a certain way
consistantly and you get a reputation for acting in that way, correct? Is
the motivation for the policy? If so, this is a very consequentialist act.

>I am not a relativist because I contend that, as human beings, many common
>conditions apply to us in determining what makes a flourishing life. The
>conditions for living well largely are not open to our choice. (If we change
>our basic biological and psychological nature in the future, some of the
>conditions for our flourishing may change.) On the other hand, I would not
>argue that ethical behavior is in any way compulsory. If someone simply
>has no interest in flourishing, there's not much you can say to them (at
>leat if they persist in their lack of interest after explaining what it is
>to flourish).

I am a relativist because, all though all of those may be true, new data
can change the way I think about how to flourish. I realize the methods I'm
using right now are probably not perfect, but simply the best I can come up
with using the available data.

>Attempting to live virtuously, rather than deciding what's right for you on
>the basis of your transitory desires, helps form an integrated, strong core
>of personality. Virtues, principles, and the projects and life-plans they
>inform, strengthen us, deepen our sense of self, and help us resist being
>pushed around by the vagaries of external forces. "What kind of person do I
>want to be?" I see as properly prior to the question "what do I most want to
>do or gain in this situation?" As a pragmatic aspect of a virtue approach,
>I claim that a concern for character and virtue over pursuit of your wants,
>will be more likely to support self-esteem, to foster healthy relationships,
>and to earn the good regard of others.

This all sounds sounds self-contradictory. If you want to be a man of
character and virtue, how can your concern for character and virtue be put
over your wants? Why would you want to be a man of character and virtue?
What's the motivation? It looks like you want the benifits of being *seen*
as a man of character and virtue. That way people will trust you and
hopefully imbue a positive reputation to you. This can lead to monetary or
social gains. Very consequentialist and mercenary if you ask me.

Don't get me wrong, I respect you for taking this angle, but lets put it
into perspective.

>On this List and elsewhere, often noted is the decline in personal
>responsibility we see in the general population. People sue one another
>to gain a quick profit, they demand benefits from the State while whining
>when it comes time to pay, and they fail to take care of their physical,
>intellectual, and psychological health. I believe that part of the cause of
>this is the subjectivist and relativist line pushed in schools (even made
>into educational theories such as "progressivism") and through much of the
>media. Whim, desire, and greed have been replacing the character and virtue
>that create and sustain strong, autonomous selves. To borrow a line, the
>world is perishing from an orgy of self-sacrificing.

I fail to see how whim, desire and greed lead to an orgy of
self-sacrificing. Virtuous living is far more commonly related to
self-sacrifice.

*I* think the major problem is short term thinking.

>Virtue ethics, with its basis in an idea of flourishing, or the good as health,
>is an ethics of self-realization. As such, it seems highly extropic.

But, to me, it appears to be imposing limits on yourself, which seems
highly un-extropic.

>Why would you object to faking a relationship if it got you what you want?
>I understand why Dave objects -- he considers it fraud (and I agree). But if,
>in the particular situation, it got you what you wanted, I presume you
>would have no problem with this either?

I thought I answered this with my comment that it violated my aesthetics.
In addition, I would say that there are good chances that long term
consequences would catch up to me if I got the habit of committing such
fraud. (Yes, I do consider it fraud. The reason I don't commit fraud
because of the probable consequences of doing so.)

Sounds a lot like what you're saying doesn't it? But I'm staying flexible
while your boxing yourself into a corner.

>Without a willingness to hold
>ethical principles and think in terms of character rather than individual
>outcomes, I don't see how you can distinguish faking relationships for
>personal advantage from using sex.

<sigh> Because the individual outcome of faking the relationship is likely
to cause an increase in one's negative reputation, and the stress and
effort of faking the relationship would cause that period of time to be a
living hell.

>> These all seem to be personal aesthetics to me. What happened to the great
>> ammoralist? [....]
>
>The great amoralist developed into something a little more subtle. My
>amoralism (as in "Morality or Reality?" in EXTROPY #1, for those who don't
>understand Geoff's remark) was a rebellion against my former Objectivist
>viewpoint. Looking that article over, I see that I still agree with most of
>what I wrote. However, what I presented as applying to _all_  morality
>I now think applied to practically all moral systems we're familiar with,
>but not to the approach I've sketched here, an approach with is neither
>consequentialist nor deontological. I continue to think that morality is
>not compulsory, and that its truths are not quite like those of the physical
>sciences.

I once had a conversation on this, about a year ago. During our dialogue,
one of us (I *thought* it was you) said something about "pre-calculating" a
lot of the thought that goes into making an ethical decision into an
ethical framework, but still making some of the calculation based on the
situation.

I liked that approach, since it still allowed for flexibility based on new
data, but gives you an ethical structure to work within. These "virtues"
seem to be a lot more static, like you made the calculation and threw away
everything but the answer. How do you deal with changing factors in your
environment with this new approach?

>You _have to_ accept the "laws" of physics, but you can choose
>whether you want to flourish as a human being. Nevertheless, _if_ you
>want to flourish -- live a self-realizing healthy existence as the kind of
>being you are -- _then_ ethical truths come into play.

Which ethical truths are you referring to? More importantly, whose ethical
truths, mine, yours, Billy Graham's or perhaps Hillary Clinton's? How do
you avoid that slippery slope?

I assume we all have to work out our own "ethical truths", but then they
aren't ethical truths are they? Ethical opinions?

Max, even though we agree on a lot of common ground here, your viewpoint
seems seriously flawed and self-limiting. I'm afraid you haven't convinced
me.

