Max More replies to Dave Krieger:
>> Hey, what's wrong with bribery?  In situations where the market has been
>> distorted by statist interference, bribery is often the only way to give
>> market forces an opportunity to reallocate the resource. [....]
>
>As a libertarian, naturally I agree with this. But what is the relevance to
>what I said? You're talking about bribery to be allowed to do what you
>should be able to do anyway. I was talking about bribery to get you something
>you otherwise would be unable to earn through your talents.

Max, frequently in your post you chide Dave for not responding directly to
your strawman attack on the original position. This branch of thread
started with talking about Madonna and alleged rise to fame by sleeping
with the right people. The music industry does *not* have a reputation for
allowing the talented to succeed as a general rule.  In that industry
perhaps more than most, it's not what you know but who you know (or perhaps
blow, if you'll pardon the crude pun). So, getting back to the original
example, instead of your rather slanted hypothetical situations, do you
condemn this woman?

One of the assumptions you seem to be making, btw, seems to be that you
cannot sell sex without losing your dignity. As a supporter of COYOTE (Call
Of Your Old Tired Ethics, the prostitute's union and activist group) and
having used such services in the past (distant pass of course, hopefully,
past the statute of limitations <grin>), I object to this assumption. I've
know sex workers whose dignity you may aspire to. I'll agree that it is
tough to do hang on to that dignity, but that's largely because of the
assumptions and attitudes similar to the ones you are showing here.

You also throw in the fraud angle, which is a seperate issue, IMHO. It is
quite possible to sell sex while being totally honest about what you are
doing and what your intentions are.

These examples you use, and parallels you draw are mystifying to me:
>Maybe this a difference in basic approach. I see sex and relationships as
>quite difficult to separate. You said you agreed with the undesirability of
>faking relationships. It could be hard to separate the two. To me, it isn't
>simply a matter of sex being a different status from other things you can
>exchange for desired ends (or exchanging your desired end for things you
>want!). I think offering something else for your goal could be just as wrong.
>For instance, if I offer to have your rival killed or their character
>publicly smeared in return for your promoting me. I'm concerned primarily
>with someone's character, the kind of person they choose to be.

and:
>> To my mind, selling sex is no different than selling any other commodity
>> you may have.
>
>Would you also sell your principles, and your friends and family? These,
>too, are commodities. If the answer is no, then you _do_ consider some
>
>aspects of your self and your relationships not as commodities. The
>difference between our attitudes would then be then I hold more things
>as non-commodities.

Are you telling me that a consentual act such as sex is related to
non-consentual acts such as murder or slavery? Should I have to explain why
selling use of one's own body is different than selling friends and
families?

I shouldn't have to, but I will: Your friends and family are commodities,
but they don't belong to you. If you sell them, it's theft. Theft of their
liberty. Your examples and your logic is fallacious.

In your reply to me, you address this point directly:
>> What does bother me is the absolutist moral sense in which his argument was
>> phrased. Referrences to cheating in sports and false relationships, etc are
>> all taking the extremes of the issue and dealing with them as if it were
>> the entire issue. [...]
>
>It seems to me that the same principles are involved in these cases. You
>haven't shown otherwise, so I think it reasonable to bring these cases into
>account. I find it useful to find parallels between situations and to develop
>principles and virtues. I believe this makes for a personality with stronger
>integration and integrity. You prefer to take each situation in isolation,
>in the manner of many contemporary ethicists.

But doesn't this fall into the same trap that the government gets mired in
when they try to make the world safe for everyone? The recent laws about
making it illegal to serve rare beef in New Jersey, because of the e-coli
incident in Washington State come to mind.

By basing your "virtues" on extreme cases aren't you limiting yourself for
the sake of avoiding highly unlikely situations? Why do this when you could
take things on a case by case basis and avoid those nasty situations as
they come up?

In all of your examples, I would make the same choices you have, but I
refuse to generalize on the basis of a few extreme examples. Experience has
tought me that that method of thinking can be dangerous.

The burden of proof, btw, is yours. You have to prove that the same
principles apply in these cases, if you're going to apply these
generalizations and expect me to take them seriously.

